text:
sam: heads-up — seeing garbled streaming responses from the /v1/stream endpoint when the client reconnects really fast. chunks arrive out-of-order and we get empty `data:` lines. Node SDK repro.

jia: is this only Node or browser too? stack + sdk version?

sam: Node 18 with sdk v0.9.2. Browser (EventSource) seems ok in quick smoke, but server-side fetch streaming hits it more. repro steps: open stream, close after first token, reopen within 50ms. sometimes tokens shuffle.

kevin: can you paste a raw chunk sequence (what you see on the wire)? especially headers: Last-Event-ID / X-Chunk-Seq if present.

sam: snippet (redacted):
```
data: {"text":"Hello"}


data: 


data: {"text":" there"}


```
order observed: Hello, there, Hello (duplicate/overlap)

ada: hmm empty data lines are often heartbeats. but duplicates + reorder means we might be replaying a cached buffer on reconnect. do we use last-event-id?

sam: Node fetch stream handler doesn't set Last-Event-ID on reconnect. browser EventSource auto-handles last-event-id.

jia: sdk parity gap then — server expects client to send Last-Event-ID header (or `?last_event_id=`) on reconnect so it can resume. Node SDK should surface lastEventId from previous stream.

kevin: also check SSE parsing: many tiny libraries collapse consecutive empty lines weirdly. if we don't consume the tail of the previous stream before reconnect, server may still be flushing queued chunks to the connection that closed; intermediaries can reorder.

sam: we saw this reproducible behind our proxy. proxy logs show "upstream closed, resumed, sent buffered chunks". could be proxy reusing connection pool.

ada: proposal: two parts — (1) SDK: emit monotonic sequence numbers on each chunk (internal only) and respect Last-Event-ID to request resume; (2) Dev docs: recommend a 200ms reconnect backoff and a quick filter for empty-data heartbeats.

jia: i can PR a Node SDK patch: keep lastEventId on stream close, and add option `resumeOnReconnect: true` (default false until tested).

kevin: small parser suggestion (for Node fetch READABLE):
```js
let buffer = '';
res.body.on('data', (c) => {
  buffer += c.toString();
  let parts = buffer.split('\n\n');
  while (parts.length > 1) {
    let frame = parts.shift();
    buffer = parts.join('\n\n');
    // parse frame safely, ignore if frame.trim() === '' (heartbeat)
  }
});
```
This avoids emitting partial frames and will drop empty frames.

sam: that helps. but ordering still weird if server resends cached tokens after late reconnect. maybe we need id-based dedupe.

ada: agree — server should attach `id` fields (or X-Chunk-Seq) to every event. SDK can then dedupe by last seen id. can we add that server-side?

kevin: server already sends `id:` when model flushes a new token but not guaranteed for heartbeats. ops: can we make the runtime attach sequence numbers to all stream messages?

deploy-bot: deploy queued: runtime/streaming-seq v1.2 -> staging at 2025-03-12T14:02Z

jia: i'll wire the Node SDK change to pick up `id` and set Last-Event-ID on reconnect. also add `streamStrictOrdering` flag so teams can opt-in to dedupe+seq enforcement.

sam: nice. can we add a troubleshooting snippet to DevEx docs about proxy timeouts and connection reuse? saved me a ton.

ada: yes — short checklist: 1) enable `streaming-seq` on runtime, 2) enable `resumeOnReconnect` in SDK, 3) client backoff 50-200ms, 4) ignore empty `data:` frames as heartbeats, 5) dedupe on event id.

kevin: i'll open infra ticket to check proxy keepalive behavior and ensure upstream connections aren't reordering buffered frames.

sam: thanks everyone — huge improvement after local patch. will post a minimal repro gist later. :thumbsup:
